# Code owners for the paths that enforce the release-generated-rootfiles model.
#
# Why this file exists: for `pull_request` events GitHub runs the workflow
# definitions from the PR's own merge commit, so a PR can rewrite the very check
# that is supposed to judge it. Nothing else stopped that — a single PR could
# replace rootfiles-guard's body with a no-op AND add a hand-authored rootfile,
# and all three required checks would report success (transpile-and-validate
# scopes by caliber source paths, which a workflow-only change does not touch).
#
# Requiring a code owner's approval on these paths means such a PR needs sign-off
# from someone who owns the enforcement machinery, not just any of the write-role
# collaborators. This only has teeth while the ruleset on `main` has
# require_code_owner_review enabled — the file and that setting are a pair.
#
# Deliberately a single owner. Consequence, by design: a PR from anyone else
# touching these paths cannot merge without @platykurtic-icu's approval. And
# because GitHub forbids self-approval, a PR *authored by* @platykurtic-icu
# touching these paths can never satisfy this rule at all — it has to go through
# the ruleset's admin bypass checkbox. That is the accepted trade for keeping
# sole ownership of the enforcement machinery; add a second owner if that
# friction stops being worth it.

# CI enforcement: the guard, the PR gate, the release producer, the shared action.
/.github/ @platykurtic-icu

# The validators and the affected-calibers rule the workflows depend on.
/scripts/ @platykurtic-icu

